        <Data name="Description"><value>A system comprising a horizontal tunnel from a spring to a collection chamber, and a 52m long stepped tunnel ascending into the fortified city, dated by the author to the 10th or 9th century BCE (early Iron Age II). A large circular stepped shaft, 11.3m in diameter and 24m deep, cut into rock with a spiral staircase, designed to access groundwater. The author dates its construction to the 7th century BCE (late Iron Age II). Mentioned as a site with an underground water system where a pulley could have been used, similar to the one depicted in an Assyrian relief, suggesting a sophisticated water supply during sieges. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with two monumental water systems, likely involving tunnels or shafts to access groundwater within the fortified city. A water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Gibeon, dating to the Iron Age II.</value></Data>
      </ExtendedData>
