    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Jerusalem (City of David) (ירושלים (עיר דוד))</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Jerusalem (City of David) (ירושלים (עיר דוד))</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Aqueduct Channel</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Judean Hills</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.6, Summary (186) (Pages 131, 132, 133, 134, 135, 136, 137, 138, 139, 140, 141, 142, 143, 144, 145, 186, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>Hezekiah&apos;s Tunnel, a 533m long rock-cut channel, diverted water from the Gihon Spring to the Pool of Siloam within the city walls, constructed in the late 8th century BCE (late Iron Age II) during King Hezekiah&apos;s reign. A large pool (15x15m during the First Temple period) located at the southern end of Hezekiah&apos;s Tunnel, serving as the primary reservoir for the diverted Gihon Spring waters within the city. Hezekiah&apos;s Tunnel, a 530m long rock-cut tunnel, was constructed in the 8th century BCE (Iron II) to divert water from the Gihon Spring to the Pool of Siloam inside the city walls, a complex engineering feat involving two teams digging from opposite ends. Warren&apos;s Shaft, a natural vertical karstic shaft (13m deep), was accidentally discovered in the 8th century BCE during preparations for Hezekiah&apos;s Tunnel, leading to attempts to connect it to the Gihon Spring and integrate it into the water system. Channel IV, an overflow channel originating near the Pool of Siloam and extending to the Kidron Valley, is considered by some scholars to be an original part of Hezekiah&apos;s Tunnel, dating its construction to the 8th century BCE. Various pools mentioned in biblical texts (e.g., Upper Pool, Lower Pool, Old Pool, Pool of Siloam) likely collected runoff water, serving as secondary water sources for the city, primarily for non-drinking uses during the Iron Age. The &apos;Hasmonean Water Channel,&apos; a rock-cut channel near the Temple Mount, collected runoff water for cisterns. Although named Hasmonean, some scholars propose it dates to the First Temple period (Iron Age). Bir Ayub, identified with biblical Ein Rogel, is a 38m deep well fed by a small cave and fissures. Its water was primarily used for irrigating fields and gardens, and its biblical mentions indicate its use during the Iron Age. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. An aqueduct system within the city, likely feeding a reservoir or pool, was present in Jerusalem during the Iron Age II. A reservoir or pool, likely fed by an aqueduct or tunnel, was present in Jerusalem during the Iron Age II.</p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
      <ExtendedData>
        <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Jerusalem (City of David)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>ירושלים (עיר דוד)</value></Data>
