    <Placemark>
      <name>Khirbet Tov (חורבת טוב)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Khirbet Tov (חורבת טוב)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Ancient City Water System</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Negev</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.16, Summary (186) (Pages 158, 186, 187, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A fortress from the 7th century BCE with a water system comprising a well (3.9m diameter and depth) located 46m from the fortress, and a channel passing through the fortress wall, likely for collecting runoff and possibly supplemented by the well. An unlocated water reservoir within the fortress is hypothesized. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. Wells were found at Khirbet Tov, which are dated with high probability to the Iron Age II period. A reservoir with an external feeding system was possibly identified at Khirbet Tov during the Iron Age II, though its identification is noted with some uncertainty.</p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
