        <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>חורבת טוב</value></Data>
        <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Ancient City Water System</value></Data>
        <Data name="Region"><value>Negev</value></Data>
        <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
        <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.16, Summary (186)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>158, 186, 187, 191</value></Data>
        <Data name="Description"><value>A fortress from the 7th century BCE with a water system comprising a well (3.9m diameter and depth) located 46m from the fortress, and a channel passing through the fortress wall, likely for collecting runoff and possibly supplemented by the well. An unlocated water reservoir within the fortress is hypothesized. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. Wells were found at Khirbet Tov, which are dated with high probability to the Iron Age II period. A reservoir with an external feeding system was possibly identified at Khirbet Tov during the Iron Age II, though its identification is noted with some uncertainty.</value></Data>
      </ExtendedData>
      <Point>
        <coordinates>35.08892,31.00039,0</coordinates>
      </Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Midas City (מידאס סיטי (פריגיה))</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Midas City (מידאס סיטי (פריגיה))</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Tunnel Shaft</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Phrygia</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.43 (Pages 177, 178, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A fortified city with several tunnels and stepped passages cut into the rock, leading to springs below the city level. These systems, including a large cave-like chamber with ventilation shafts and a fountain, provided hidden access to water during sieges. A water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Midas City, dating to the Iron Age II.</p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
      <ExtendedData>
