        <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>מידאס סיטי (פריגיה)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Tunnel Shaft</value></Data>
        <Data name="Region"><value>Phrygia</value></Data>
        <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
        <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.43</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>177, 178, 191</value></Data>
        <Data name="Description"><value>A fortified city with several tunnels and stepped passages cut into the rock, leading to springs below the city level. These systems, including a large cave-like chamber with ventilation shafts and a fountain, provided hidden access to water during sieges. A water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Midas City, dating to the Iron Age II.</value></Data>
