    <Placemark>
      <name>Nineveh (נינוה (אשור))</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Nineveh (נינוה (אשור))</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Aqueduct Channel</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Mesopotamia</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.46, 2.9.52 (Pages 180, 181, 185, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>Sennacherib (704-681 BCE) built extensive water systems for Nineveh, including the Kisiri Canal (16km) from the Khosr river, diverting 18 streams from the Bashiqah mountains, and the Hinis Canal (55km) from the Gomel river basin, which crossed rivers via bridges (like Jerwan) and mountains via tunnels. An Assyrian relief from the time of Ashurbanipal (668-631 BCE) depicts an aqueduct on pointed arches, similar to the Jerwan aqueduct built by Sennacherib, bringing water to a flourishing garden near Nineveh. An aqueduct bringing water from a distance outside the city was found at Nineveh, dating to the Iron Age II.</p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
      <ExtendedData>
        <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Nineveh</value></Data>
        <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>נינוה (אשור)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Aqueduct Channel</value></Data>
        <Data name="Region"><value>Mesopotamia</value></Data>
        <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
        <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.46, 2.9.52</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>180, 181, 185, 191</value></Data>
        <Data name="Description"><value>Sennacherib (704-681 BCE) built extensive water systems for Nineveh, including the Kisiri Canal (16km) from the Khosr river, diverting 18 streams from the Bashiqah mountains, and the Hinis Canal (55km) from the Gomel river basin, which crossed rivers via bridges (like Jerwan) and mountains via tunnels. An Assyrian relief from the time of Ashurbanipal (668-631 BCE) depicts an aqueduct on pointed arches, similar to the Jerwan aqueduct built by Sennacherib, bringing water to a flourishing garden near Nineveh. An aqueduct bringing water from a distance outside the city was found at Nineveh, dating to the Iron Age II.</value></Data>
      </ExtendedData>
