    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Arad (תל ערד)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Arad (תל ערד)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Ancient City Water System</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Negev</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.15, Summary (186) (Pages 155, 156, 157, 158, 186, 187, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A water system for the fortress, including a deep well (25.19m deep, 4.6m diameter) outside the walls, and a covered rock-cut channel (24m long) leading under the city wall to three large plastered cisterns (approx. 250 cubic meters) inside the fortress. The system collected runoff and was supplemented by water from the well. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. Wells were found at Tel Arad, which are dated with high probability to the Iron Age II period. A reservoir with an external feeding system was identified at Tel Arad during the Iron Age II.</p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
