    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Aroer (תל ערוער)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Aroer (תל ערוער)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Ancient City Water System</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Negev</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.17, Summary (186) (Pages 159, 186, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A walled settlement from the 7th century BCE with a large, unexcavated, bowl-shaped depression (40m diameter, 6m deep) within the walls, hypothesized to be a subterranean water reservoir fed by floodwaters from Nahal Aroer. Additionally, wells outside the settlement were likely used. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. A well was found at Tel Aroer, dated to the Iron Age II period. A reservoir with an external feeding system was possibly identified at Tel Aroer during the Iron Age II, though its identification is noted with some uncertainty.</p>]]></description>
