    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Beit Shemesh (תל בית שמש)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Beit Shemesh (תל בית שמש)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Reservoir</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Shephelah</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.9, 2.9.26, 2.9.27, Summary (186) (Pages 144, 145, 146, 164, 165, 186, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A monumental, cruciform-shaped rock-cut reservoir (24x23.5m, 6m high) in Area C, fed by multiple channels collecting runoff, was constructed in the 10th century BCE (Iron IIA) and reused in the 7th century BCE. A round cistern in Area B, dated to Iron Age II, measuring 5.75m in diameter and 7m deep. It features a 0.6m diameter opening, a 5m neck (3m built, 2m rock-cut), and 10cm thick gray plaster. Its estimated volume is 87 cubic meters. A rock-cut, spherical cistern in Area A, dated to Iron Age II, with a 1x1m square opening and a 1.5m neck, reaching a depth of 4.5m. Its volume is approximately 15 cubic meters (excluding the neck), with traces of disintegrated plaster found. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, specifically mentioning a cruciform-shaped reservoir designed to maximize storage volume and prevent roof collapse. A reservoir or pool was found at Tel Beit Shemesh, dated to the Iron Age II period. Cisterns or pools were found at Tel Beit Shemesh, dated to the Iron Age II period.</p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
