    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Gezer (תל גזר)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Gezer (תל גזר)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Reservoir</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Shephelah</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.8, Summary (186) (Pages 143, 186, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A large, double rock-cut reservoir, approximately 18m deep with plastered walls, was likely filled by runoff water. The excavator dates this installation to the Iron Age. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. A reservoir or pool was found at Tel Gezer, dated to the Iron Age II period. Cisterns or pools were found at Tel Gezer, dated to the Iron Age II period.</p>]]></description>
