    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Yokneam (תל יקנעם)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Yokneam (תל יקנעם)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Tunnel Shaft</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Jezreel Valley</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.2, Summary (186) (Pages 121, 186, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A proposed water system, similar to Hazor&apos;s, is indicated by an oval-shaped pit carved into the rock in the lowest part of the city, near external springs. The city&apos;s casemate wall, built in the 10th century BCE (Iron Age IIA), incorporates this feature, suggesting a shaft leading to groundwater. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. A water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Tel Yokneam, dating to the Iron Age II.</p>]]></description>
